{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And they said, etc. — let the land be given, etc.</b> It was necessary to say the word <b>וַיֹּאמְרוּ</b> even though they were still speaking and there was no break in their words, to alert you that until now they had been arranging the proposal in such a way that through it their request would be accepted, in the manner I explained above; and from here begins the statement of the request itself. And their saying <b>let it be given</b> rather than 'you shall give' — in accordance with what I explained, that the power of inheritance of the land resides in king, priest, and Israel; therefore they said ״יֻתַּן,״ meaning, let the giving be in a manner that is effective — either if Moses wishes to give on his own, according to the prerogative of a king who may give, as I have written, or with the agreement of Elazar and the princes, according to the law of the division of the land." "<b>Do not make us cross the Jordan.</b> The intention here is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Bava Batra 109a), concerning the verse ״וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם אֶת נַחֲלָתוֹ לְבִתּוֹ״ — that the daughter transfers the inheritance from its place. So too they intended to say that if he does not give them the land of their possession that is fitting for them, behold he is transferring their inheritance from its place." "<b>Further,</b> they intended to remove the stumbling block of suspicion against them — that they were choosing this land for the sake of ease, so as not to enter the land of their enemies. And they said that the reason for their words is only because they do not wish to take their inheritance across the Jordan but rather here, on account of the preparation they have there; and not in order to spare themselves the trouble of the wars against the inhabitants of the land, for they have no part in that except the crossing itself." "<b>Further,</b> they intended by the statement <b>do not make us cross</b> to say that there is in this something beneficial for Israel, that it would lighten the yoke from upon them and they would not need to apportion them land in the Land." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that they intended to remove the condition that they cross with Israel, in the manner that Moses ultimately imposed upon them. And they relied on the reason they gave at the beginning of their words when they said ״אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳,״ and there is no need for every armed man to cross."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:37.095776180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}